    id: "d08838652c60db99cc35",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Ethiopia’s PM insists on access to Red Sea despite regional conflict threat",
    summary: "Neighbouring countries say coastal states must govern Red Sea as fighting escalates in northern Ethiopia.",
    preview: "Ethiopia’s Abiy demands access to Red Sea as tensions with neighbours rise Neighbouring countries say coastal states must govern Red Sea as fighting escalates in northern Ethiopia. Ethiopia’s Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed has insisted that his landlocked country must have access to the Red Sea, even as neighbouring countries have said responsibility for the sea rests with the states along its shores. Abiy made the demand as he was sworn in for another five-year term in power on Monday.",
    body: "Ethiopia’s Abiy demands access to Red Sea as tensions with neighbours rise\n\nNeighbouring countries say coastal states must govern Red Sea as fighting escalates in northern Ethiopia.\n\nEthiopia’s Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed has insisted that his landlocked country must have access to the Red Sea, even as neighbouring countries have said responsibility for the sea rests with the states along its shores.\n\nAbiy made the demand as he was sworn in for another five-year term in power on Monday.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 4 items\n\n- list 1 of 4Ethiopian gov’t-aligned group claims close to full control of Tigray region\n\n- list 2 of 4Ethiopian gov’t forces advance in Tigray as rebels retreat: What to know\n\n- list 3 of 4Somali president: Ethiopia can access our port, but must negotiate\n\n- list 4 of 4Ethiopia’s forces retake Mekelle amid renewed Tigray fighting\n\nRepeating previous assertions, Abiy said in a speech in Parliament that the country would work to secure a port on the Red Sea, saying that access was critical to Ethiopia’s “civilisation”.\n\n“Based on the principle of give and take, we will work diligently to ensure that Ethiopia has an outlet to the sea,” he said.\n\nEthiopia lost direct access to the Red Sea in 1993 after Eritrea, formerly a region of Ethiopia, became independent.\n\nAbiy’s call came as Ethiopia’s relations its neighbours have worsened in recent months, with Addis Ababa accusing Eritrea, Egypt and Sudan of supporting opposition forces in the northern Tigray region, following a resurgence of the fighting against Abiy’s government there.\n\nFor its part, Eritrea has accused Ethiopia of seeking access to the sea through its territory, while a separate dispute over Ethiopia’s Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Nile River has strained relations with Egypt and Sudan, which rely on the river for water.\n\nFour of the five countries bordering the Red Sea – Egypt, Eritrea, Sudan and Somalia – met on Sunday in Egypt’s New Alamein and called for cooperation on regional issues.\n\nThey said governance of the Red Sea was the exclusive responsibility of its coastal states and rejected actions that could affect Egypt’s and Sudan’s use of Nile waters.\n\nThe following day, Ethiopia’s Foreign Ministry said discussions over the Red Sea must account for the country’s “legitimate interests and enduring connection” to the body of water.\n\nSomalia’s president, Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, who attended the Egypt summit, congratulated Abiy on his re-election.\n\n“I look forward to working closely together to strengthen Somalia-Ethiopia relations & advance peace, stability & shared prosperity for our peoples & the Horn of Africa,” he said in a post on X.\n\nMohamud had previously told Al Jazeera that Somalia did not object to Ethiopia gaining access to the Red Sea through Somali territory but added that the two countries would need to officially negotiate.\n\nEvariste Ndayishimiye, Burundi’s president, also wished Abiy “every success during his renewed mandate” while Mahmoud Ali Youssouf, the chairperson of the African Union commission, also sent a message of congratulations.",
